course: technology
lesson: 8
title: How a car is built
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A car factory works like a giant recipe: flat steel sheets go in at one end and a finished car drives out at the other, in a modern factory about every minute or two.
explain: First huge presses squash the steel into doors and roofs in one blow, then swarms of robot arms join the pieces with thousands of spot welds while sparks fly.
explain: The bare body is dipped against rust, painted by robots in dust-free rooms, and then crawls along the assembly line while people and robots add the engine, wheels, seats, wires and windows.
explain: Every new car is started, driven a little and sprayed with water to check for leaks, and some cars are crashed on purpose with dummies full of sensors so engineers can make cars safer.
explain: One car holds parts from dozens of suppliers in many countries, which is why in 2021 a shortage of tiny computer chips stopped car factories around the world.
fact: In the press shop huge presses squash flat steel sheets into doors, roofs and bonnets in one hard blow; they are fenced off and stop if a light beam is broken.
fact: Robot arms join the steel pieces with several thousand spot welds to build the bare car body; welding light can hurt eyes, so welding happens behind screens.
fact: A car body is dipped in a bath that protects it against rust, then robots spray paint and clear coat in dust-free rooms and ovens bake it hard.
fact: Paint shops are kept dust-free because one speck under the paint shows as a bump.
fact: On the final assembly line the painted body moves slowly while workers and robots add the engine, wheels, seats, wires and windows, each doing one job; a modern factory can finish a car about every minute or two.
fact: Crash-test dummies full of sensors ride in new cars that are crashed on purpose, and they come in sizes including children and babies; groups like Euro NCAP publish safety ratings.
fact: Crash tests are why every seat has a belt and children need the right car seat or booster for their size, so the belt sits across them safely.
fact: A windscreen is two layers of glass with a plastic sheet between, so when it cracks the pieces stick together in a spider-web pattern instead of flying.
fact: Car side windows are toughened glass that breaks into small blunt pieces.
fact: Tyres are rubber mixed with carbon black, built in layers around steel and fabric cords and baked in a mould that presses the tread; the carbon black makes them black and stronger.
fact: Every new car is started, driven a little, tested on rollers and sprayed with water in a rain booth to check for leaks before it leaves.
fact: New cars travel to dealers on double-deck car transporter lorries, and car ships can carry several thousand cars at once.
fact: One car has parts from dozens of suppliers in different countries, delivered just in time, and a shortage of computer chips in 2021 stopped car factories around the world.
exercise: What happens in a car factory press shop? | Huge presses squash steel sheets into doors and roofs | Workers hammer every single door into shape by hand with mallets | Melted steel is poured into a mould the shape of a whole car | Old cars are squashed flat there so the steel can be scrapped
exercise: Why does a press stop if its light beam is broken? | So it cannot come down while someone is near it | To save electricity whenever nobody is standing and watching it | To check that the steel sheet is shiny enough before pressing | To count how many doors the machine has made so far today
exercise: How is a car body joined together? | Robot arms weld it with thousands of spot welds | It is stuck together with paper glue | It is sewn together with thick steel thread on huge machines | One worker bolts the whole body together in a single day
exercise: Why does welding happen behind screens? | Welding light can hurt eyes | The sparks are kept a secret | The screens keep the robots warm | The screens make the welds stronger
exercise: Why is a car body dipped in a bath before painting? | To protect it against rust | To make it lighter | To cool it down after the oven | To wash the robots' fingerprints off
exercise: Why must a car paint shop be dust-free? | One speck under the paint shows as a bump | Dust makes wet paint catch fire and explode | Dust turns every colour of paint grey | Dust makes the painting robots stop
exercise: How often can a modern factory finish a car? | About every minute or two | About once a month | About once a day, by the evening | About ten every second
exercise: What happens on the final assembly line? | Engine, wheels, seats and windows are added as the body moves along | Old cars are taken apart and sorted into metal for scrap | Flat steel sheets are pressed into doors, roofs and bonnets | Each car is designed and drawn by hand on big sheets of paper
exercise: Why are some new cars crashed on purpose? | To learn from sensor dummies how to make cars safer for people | To make the paint look worn and old for the films | Because those cars came out wrong and must be thrown away | To find out how loud a crash is for people nearby
exercise: What sizes do crash-test dummies come in? | Adult, child and baby sizes | Only one size, a grown man | Only giant sizes, bigger than people | Only tiny toy sizes, like dolls
exercise: Why does a windscreen crack like a spider web instead of falling in? | A plastic sheet between two layers of glass holds the pieces | It is made of clear plastic only, so it cannot shatter | It has thin metal wires running all the way through it | The glass is frozen so hard it cannot fall apart
exercise: How do car side windows break? | Into small blunt pieces | Into long sharp splinters like knives | They can never break, whatever hits them | Into fine sand that blows away
exercise: Why are car tyres black? | Carbon black is mixed in, making them stronger | Rubber is already black when it comes out of the tree | They are painted black at the very end of the line | They turn black from the dirt on the test track
exercise: How does a factory check a new car for leaks? | It sprays it with water in a rain booth | It drives it into the sea for a minute | It fills the inside with sand | It waits for the first owner to complain
exercise: How do new cars travel across the sea? | On car ships that carry several thousand cars | Driven through long tunnels under every ocean | Flown by helicopter one at a time | Floated across on their own tyres, pulled by tugs
exercise: Why did a shortage of tiny chips in 2021 stop car factories? | Modern cars need computer chips and can't be finished without them | Chips are burnt as fuel in car engines, so the cars had nothing to run on | Car paint is made from ground-up chips | Chips are what hold the seats on
exercise: A car transporter carries 8 cars with 4 wheels each. How many car wheels is it carrying? | 32 | 12 | 24 | 36
test: «robots build the whole car, no people at all, right?» What is right? | Robots do much welding and painting, but people still build and check | Yes, not one person works in any car factory any more | No, people still build cars entirely by hand with no machines | Robots only carry the coffee and post around there
test: «crash-test dummies are just shop mannequins» What is right? | They are full of sensors that measure what a crash would do to people | Yes, plain shop dummies in clothes, just heavier | They are robots that sit in and drive the cars | They are statues kept at the factory for decoration
test: «windscreen got hit by a stone, cracked like a web but didn't fall in, weird» Why? | The plastic layer between two sheets of glass holds the pieces | The glass was frozen at the time, so it held | The stone was too small to really hurt the glass | The windscreen is not real glass, just thin plastic
test: What would happen if a speck of dust landed on a car while it was being painted? | It would show as a bump in the paint | It would make the paint shinier | It would be painted over and vanish for good | It would make the whole car rust at once
test: «why's my little bro need a booster seat, he's fine with the belt» What is the safe answer? | Children need the right seat so the belt fits them safely | Booster seats are only there to make the ride comfier | A seat belt fits everyone the same, big or small | Boosters only matter on motorways, not in town
test: Each car has 4 wheels and a spare tyre. How many tyres for 5 cars? | 25 | 20 | 9 | 21
test: «car factory makes like 1 car a week tops» What is right? | A modern factory can finish a car about every minute or two | Yes, one car a week is the most any factory can do | It takes a whole year of work to finish each car | A thousand cars roll out of every factory every single minute
test: «saw them hosing new cars at the factory, are they just mucky?» What is most likely? | It is a rain booth test for leaks | They are washed to look nice for the shop window | The water cools the hot engines down | The cars are being painted with coloured water
test: A transporter carries 8 cars. How many transporters are needed for 40 cars? | 5 | 4 | 32 | 48
test: «tyres are black cos of road dirt, they start off white» What is right? | Carbon black is mixed in at the factory, making them stronger | Yes, tyres start off white and only get black from road dirt | They are dyed black just for style, like shoes | Rubber trees give black rubber, so it stays that way
test: In a cartoon a giant press squashes a cat flat and it pops back up. What is the real rule? | Presses are fenced off and stop if a light beam is broken | Presses are soft inside and can't hurt anyone | Workers stand under the presses to check them | Presses only squash cardboard, never anything solid
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the construction of a car windscreen? | Two layers of glass enclose a plastic sheet which retains the fragments upon cracking | A single sheet of toughened glass, which shatters into numerous small blunt pieces upon impact | The windscreen is composed entirely of a transparent plastic | Steel wires are embedded throughout the glass to bind it
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the crash tests performed upon new vehicles. | To ascertain, by instrumented dummies, the injuries occupants would sustain | To determine the noise produced by a collision at speed | To dispose of defective vehicles in a controlled manner | To test the durability of the paint under severe impact
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the year 2021. | A shortage of computer chips halted car production in factories around the world | Car factories throughout the world ceased to employ robots and returned to building vehicles by hand | Tyres were first manufactured with carbon black for strength | The first spot weld was performed by a robot in a car factory
exam: Determine the aggregate number of tyres required for six cars, it being given that each car is fitted with four wheels and one spare. | Thirty | Twenty-four | Ten | Twenty-five
